    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coral was originally called 'Oklahoma' before 1902, when a prospector claimed its coal and clay would shine like coral.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://homercenterhistori.wixsite.com/website/graceton-coral",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first coke ovens in Indiana County were built by George Mikesell in 1887, sparking Coral’s early industrial boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://homercenterhistori.wixsite.com/website/graceton-coral",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By the late 1800s, Coral’s coke plant swelled to 202 ovens, making it a regional coal powerhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://homercenterhistori.wixsite.com/website/graceton-coral",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Coral lies between Homer City and Black Lick on U.S. Route 22, with Graceton directly to its north.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area now called Graceton was once named 'Ranson' before the railroad prompted a new name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://homercenterhistori.wixsite.com/website/graceton-coral",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mikesell’s firebrick ovens were fired in Black Lick by Meldron and Company, linking Coral to regional industrial craft.",
